{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low-bias: forecasts are typically below realized prices by ~0.5–2.5 EUR for much of the horizon, especially during rising regimes. Apply an upward level shift (+~1.0 to +1.5 EUR) when the last 5–10 days show a positive slope.",
    "Underreaction to upside momentum (lag): after an upswing starts, the path is too flat and peaks arrive too low. Increase day 1–15 forecasts more than day 16–30 (front-load the correction).",
    "Over-forecasting during pullbacks: when the market transitions from a local high into a decline, the model stays too high for ~1–2 weeks. If last 3–5 days are down, reduce day 1–10 forecasts (~0.5–1.5 EUR).",
    "Excessive mean-reversion at long horizons: the model drifts back toward mid-60s while outcomes remain elevated (high-60s/low-70s). Damp mean reversion beyond ~day 15; keep forecasts closer to the recent 10-day average level.",
    "Missed late-horizon upside tails: in strong bull regimes, day 20–30 outcomes can jump (e.g., into 71–73) while forecasts remain capped ~68–69. Add a convexity/uplift term to day 20–30 when recent volatility is rising and price is above the 20-day mean."
  ]
}
